SERHAT UNSAL: We're
going to take you

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.880
through a short journey
in the next 50 minutes.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.980
So sit back and relax in these
beautifully comfortable chairs.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.810
And this is going to start
with a background on Dawn Foods

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.300
and how this 100-year-old
manufacturing

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.985
company became a global
leader in the bakery industry.

00:00:25.985 --> 00:00:28.360
And we're going to look at
some of the strategic insights

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.850
and the business case
that led into the thinking

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.430
of digital
transformation at Dawn.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.180
Then Sam is going
to take us through,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.340
together with our
partner [INAUDIBLE] here,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.680
on the specifics
of this journey,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.980
what the journey looks like,
and how the end product will

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.400
look like, what the
technical architecture is.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.580
And towards the end, we're going
to provide you with a demo,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.340
if everything goes
well as planned.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.230
It's going to be basically a
showcase for the possible, what

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.060
we can do with this system.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.190
And at the very end,
last 10 minutes,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.040
we're going to take
your questions.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.560
And our chief digital
officer, Bob Howland

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.830
is here with us today.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.650
So we're going to
invite, kindly,

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.060
Bob to join us
with the questions.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.150
So I don't know if you've
ever heard of Dawn Foods.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.570
It's one of the best kept
secrets of the business world.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.930
It's a family-owned,
privately-owned company

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.150
that's been in
business since 1920,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.910
so almost 100 years,
headquartered out

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.110
of Jackson, Michigan.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.790
It's a bakery
ingredients and bakery

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.160
products manufacturing company.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.880
And what started as a small
donut shop in 1920 in Jackson

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.060
evolved into this $2
billion global company

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.880
with more than 4,000
people working for it,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.970
operating in more
than 100 countries,

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.950
serving 40,000
customers every day.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.730
And we have more
than 650 people who

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.790
are touching these
customers on a daily basis.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.890
And Dawn operates in all
regions of the world,

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.990
but we are much bigger
in North America

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.157
where we have a unique
business model, which

00:02:06.157 --> 00:02:07.240
we're going to talk about.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.770
Because in North America,
we're not only a manufacturer,

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.610
but we are also a wholesale
distributor of bakery goods

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.280
and bakery supplies into
the baking industry, which

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.130
is vertically integrated to
our manufacturing business.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:26.290
And what we do is we not only
manufacture our products,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.140
but we distribute these
products to the end user

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.110
with our own logistics
systems together

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.570
with a bunch of other
products that are sourced

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.950
from third-party suppliers.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:42.317
We have about 30 distribution
centers across North America.

00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:43.650
And these distribution centers--

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:50.540
these distribution centers
serve about 20,000 customers

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.890
every day across North America.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.140
What do we sell?

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.440
I mean, we really sell
anything that a bakery needs,

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.010
but we can group these things
into five categories, roughly.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.540
We sell non-bakery
foods like your nuts,

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.490
raisins, cream cheese.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.030
We sell processed
bakery ingredients

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:15.000
like a donut mix, a cake mix,
a brownie mix, jams, icings.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.990
We sell raw ingredients like
sugar, flour, shortening, oil.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.330
We sell semi-finished
and finished products,

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.100
like a decorated cake in a box.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.995
If you shopped at Walmart,
bakery products there

00:03:25.995 --> 00:03:28.570
are made by Dawn, most of them.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:32.370
So we also sell non-food
products and services.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.170
We sell baking equipment.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.200
We sell pots, pans, gloves,
forks, knives, napkins,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.850
you name it, anything
that a bakery might need.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:44.820
We have more than 5,000
products in our portfolio.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.780
So we operate in two
different business

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.840
models across North America.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.300
One business model is the key
account business model, where

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.180
we take Dawn
manufactured products

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.340
like a cake mix or
an icing, and we

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.570
sell it directly to a large key
account like Walmart, 7-Eleven,

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.780
McDonald's, Panera, you name it.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.600
And in this model, because
the customers are quite large,

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.090
they order in very
big quantities.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.360
When Walmart orders
icing for their cakes,

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.539
they order like five
truckloads of icing.

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:20.000
So in this model,
we ship the products

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.520
directly from our plants
into the warehouses

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.480
of these large customers, which
is a simple business model.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.910
Yet, we have a second
business model,

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.610
where we basically serve the
small and midsize accounts.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.510
These are your neighborhood
bakeries or small donut shops

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.060
that are around the corner
in your neighborhood.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.210
And these customers,
we sell them

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.180
the Dawn products, but also the
third-party sourced products.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.900
And we consolidate all of these
things in our distribution

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.480
centers and deliver
them everyday.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.963
And their orders are, by nature,
smaller and more complex.

00:04:53.963 --> 00:04:55.130
There's a bag of this in it.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.690
There's a pail of that in it.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.370
And we need to
consolidate and make

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.770
very frequent small shipments
every day across North America.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.000
So these are the two
business models by and large.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.200
And while both business models
are very relevant and important

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.530
for Dawn, we do have a
much bigger heritage,

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.440
a well-invested
network, and also

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.300
a sustainable, competitive edge
on the small to midsize account

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.700
segment.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.180
So I mean, this is the
bulk of our customer base.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:28.390
And just like any other company,
this is more profitable for us

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.000
because these small customers
don't have the negotiation

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.520
power of a Walmart
or a McDonald's.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.400
So this is why it's
strategically imperative for us

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.160
that we protect and grow
this segment of the market.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.510
But how do we do that?

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:45.990
So as we think about how to
grow this segment of the market,

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.450
we had two separate
considerations,

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.640
or we looked at our business
through two different lenses.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:55.070
One lens we used was the
product innovation lens.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:58.520
Just like any other company,
we need to constantly innovate

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.740
our product portfolio
so we become relevant

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.210
and we differentiate ourselves.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.280
And we do that.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.250
I mean, we just introduced
gluten-free concept,

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:10.340
natural colors, natural
flavors, different formats,

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:11.700
and all that.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.210
The problem is, we have 5,000
products in the portfolio.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.350
So it's very difficult
to differentiate yourself

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.020
based on product because
every single product is not

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.270
significant enough
to make a difference.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.550
It's a small fraction
of total sales.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.130
That's why we just
looked at our business,

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:31.070
and we said, well, while
we have to innovate still,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.680
we have to bring new bakery
products to the market, that's

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.330
just table stakes.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.300
And we have to think about
a bigger mechanism, a higher

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.080
mechanism above product level to
differentiate ourselves almost

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.680
like a business model
innovation, an innovation

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.190
where the customer experience,
Dawn customer experience,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.220
will be better if
we deliver a more

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:57.210
enjoyable and simplified Dawn
experience for our customers.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.065
So we thought we need to
differentiate at that level.

00:07:00.065 --> 00:07:01.940
The other lens we looked
at our business with

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.740
was the undeniable
factor that there

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.760
is a huge digital transformation
that's taking around us

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.740
in the food service and
bakery industries which

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.420
we are part of.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.470
We see online ordering coming
into play more and more

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.030
every day with food
service distributors.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.120
Amazon, they made
a public statement

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.680
with their B2B business model.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.670
They said, we're going to go
after food service and bakery

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:26.810
industries.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.180
And our direct
manufacturing competitors

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.800
are doing a lot
of digital things

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.160
to differentiate themselves.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.400
So we just looked at
that, and we said,

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.080
we don't want to be left behind.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.180
We actually have the opportunity
to be ahead of the curve

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.560
and preempt our peers if we
act fast in this digital area

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.040
because the digital
transformation in bakery

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.730
and food service industries
are still at their infancy.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.710
So putting these
two things together,

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.710
the need to differentiate
and the need to stay

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.230
ahead of the curve,
we said, we're

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.600
going to invest in
digital technologies.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.300
And we're going to embark
on a digital transformation

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.350
journey in partnership with
KPMG with the primary goal

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.860
to give a better customer
experience to Dawn customers,

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.780
hence, differentiate ourselves.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:19.010
So of course, it all starts
with understanding our customer,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:21.320
who the customer is,
and what they want.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:24.320
And our customers are, as
I said, your neighborhood

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.530
bakeries, the donut shops.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:29.240
And there's usually
a small venue

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.270
in which there is one
decision maker, the baker.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:36.320
And this baker, by
definition, is an artist.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.429
He or she is having a
lot of fun and pride,

00:08:40.429 --> 00:08:43.400
and enjoying the art of baking.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.800
They focus on the
passion of baking,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.920
and they focus on the quality
and the taste of the food.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.260
But while it's a
small establishment,

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.360
it's a very complex
operation, a bakery.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.820
So there are a lot of other
elements of their business

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.680
that they need a lot of help.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.010
And they rely on their suppliers
to provide those helps.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:05.060
So that was one
thing we measured.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.480
At Dawn, we constantly
look at what bakers need,

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.980
and what are some of
their biggest pain points?

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.400
What are their biggest
concerns, hopes, and fears?

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.530
And here's our latest research.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.160
We understand that
the bakers, they

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.530
know technology is going
to be an important thing

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.190
in their lives.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.850
It's going to play an important
role, but they don't know how.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.600
And they don't know
how to make it happen.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.490
So they need help.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.100
In terms of product,
bakers want to make sure

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.290
that when they put
a product portfolio

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:35.660
and assortment in front
of their shoppers,

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.210
they want to make sure that
it is something that is driven

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:42.290
from the latest trends, latest
taste profiles, color profiles,

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.400
and the consumers will enjoy it.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.330
And they also want to access
the highest quality bakery

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:50.270
ingredients at the lowest price
possible to make that happen.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.480
And they need supported
recipes, et cetera.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.620
Labor is probably their
biggest pain point

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.360
because they struggle to
find, recruit, train, retrain,

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.520
and retain skilled labor.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.570
There's a huge shortage.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:07.010
Financial, as I said,
bakers are artists.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:09.530
They don't necessarily
immediately know

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:11.570
where they make money,
where they don't, or how

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.590
to prioritize their investments.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.190
So they appreciate
a lot of consulting

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.170
in the area of financial.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:21.470
Again, direct marketing,
targeting a certain consumer

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.320
group with the right products
and understanding their needs

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.490
and trends is not necessarily
an inherent skill set

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:30.920
that exists within bakeries.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.980
And then last but not the least,
regulatory and compliance,

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.070
which is all about food law,
the labeling requirements, which

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.477
is constantly changing.

00:10:38.477 --> 00:10:40.310
Bakers just want to
make sure they're, first

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.500
of all, compliant,
but they also have

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.510
access to audit
tools, certifications

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.845
which is required to
run their businesses.

00:10:47.845 --> 00:10:51.380
So I mean, while these
are very specific

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:55.390
needs that we have identified,
the overarching theme is, yes,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.010
they expect products from us.

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:00.550
They expect icings, and
cake mixes, and everything.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.670
But they want a more
holistic help from us

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:06.820
to help with their business
and with the elements

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.390
of the business that are not
necessarily related to baking.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:16.600
So combining this ambition to
go digital and the understanding

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.270
of the customers,
we just came up

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.740
with this vision
that says, we're

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.890
going to create a
digital bakery platform,

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:26.230
which we're going
to see the demo of.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.310
And through that
bakery platform,

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:31.060
we just want to make
sure we provide products

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.310
online to our
customers, but we also

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:35.680
want to surround
them with services

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.990
on a digital
environment that they're

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.830
going to be needing to run their
businesses more effectively,

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.250
hence, differentiate ourselves.

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:47.620
So as a working charter,
this digital bakery platform

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:51.400
that we're going to
show you today is--

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.680
that we want to be an
indispensable partner

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:54.370
with the bakers.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:57.130
And we want to create
a sustainable advantage

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.130
through the use of
technology and data

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:02.500
that otherwise they
wouldn't have access to.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.790
And by doing that,
we want to help them.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:07.810
And we want to help them achieve
their goals and aspirations.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:10.520
So that's what we've
been working on.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:14.250
So what does this
platform look like?

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.420
Conceptually imagine this
is a website or an app.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.070
It may take different
shapes, but this

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.710
is for explanation purposes.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.500
At the center of
it, there's going

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.990
to be a B2B product
online ordering facility

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.630
where they can basically
place their orders

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.050
and get their products online.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.890
While online ordering
is nothing new--

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.530
it's been around for
20 years by now--

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:39.870
there's very little of
that in bakery industry.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:44.410
So it's still very progressive,
simply because bakers are not

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:45.755
digital native people.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.940
The operation is very
complex, product portfolio

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.920
is very fragmented, and pricing
is very customized per baker,

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.240
per product.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.270
So there's an
inherent complexity,

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.470
which we are working on.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:03.830
Once we get that rolling,
the second thing-- so these

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.800
are areas for bakers to
go into, conceptually.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.340
We want to work on--

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:12.020
we are actually working on a
B2C online ordering facility

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.800
for our bakers.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.430
The bakers want to engage
digitally with their consumers,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:17.300
with their shoppers.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.842
They want to make
sure that they have

00:13:18.842 --> 00:13:21.385
an online ordering facility
through their stores,

00:13:21.385 --> 00:13:23.010
but they don't know
how to get it done.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.640
So we're working on
a turnkey solution

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:27.890
to provide to the
bakers so that they

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:32.510
can turn their own operation
into a digital, online-friendly

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:33.590
operation.

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:36.620
And they're going
to appreciate that.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:40.020
We said labor is a big
issue for our bakers.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.600
We are going to be connecting
to customized job search

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:48.950
engines in partnership
with Indeed, Monster,

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:53.480
or someone like ZipRecruiter
so that the bakers are

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.180
seeing who is available
and who they can recruit.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.670
We also will connect
them to training videos

00:13:58.670 --> 00:13:59.835
through YouTube.

00:13:59.835 --> 00:14:02.210
And we're going to connect
them to certification programs

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:06.940
through educational
institutions.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:08.680
In terms of marketing
and innovation,

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:12.610
at Dawn we have tremendous
knowledge of consumer desires,

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:14.920
wants, needs,
trends, everything,

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.480
and their behavioral
statistics and all that.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:19.630
So we want to make
sure that we provide

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:22.870
these things to our bakers
in a digestible format that

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.750
makes sense to them and helps
them get the right product

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:26.890
assortment.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:30.250
And also, we want to partner
with tools like Twilio.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:31.750
We're going to be
helping the bakers

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.370
to run digital campaigns,
sales campaigns, which is

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:35.620
going to increase their sales.

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:38.420
And that's what they want.

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.637
We talked about
regulatory compliance.

00:14:40.637 --> 00:14:42.220
There are a lot of
companies out there

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.730
that help companies
do these things.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:50.140
For example, TrackMyRisks.com is
one of those digital companies

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:54.220
who are specialized in auditing,
audit tools, certification.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:57.310
We want to work with them on
a bakery-specific solution

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:59.980
and plug them to our platform.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.440
Same with financial, lots
of financial companies

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:05.470
who can help us with training,
measuring tools, investment

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:09.177
prioritization tools that we
can plug onto the platform.

00:15:09.177 --> 00:15:11.260
And one of the things that
we're going to be doing

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.440
is in the side of operations,
think about a neighborhood

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.380
baker receiving a
large donut order,

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.990
like 500 donuts
for a college party

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:21.547
in the middle of the night.

00:15:21.547 --> 00:15:23.630
Well, what they need to
do is, of course, make it,

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:24.850
and box it, and everything.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.160
But to deliver it,
they usually have

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.070
a van, which is sometimes out.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.170
So they need to wait for
that van to come back.

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:34.840
And the order is
delayed and all that.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:37.750
Instead, we want to connect
them to a last mile delivery

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.480
service like
Postmates, Curbside,

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:43.750
so they don't need
that van anymore maybe.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:46.860
They can just deliver their
product through those services.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:48.360
By doing those
things, they're going

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.890
to be able to look at their
business and their assets

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:52.750
very differently.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:56.020
And we can go on and
on, and be as creative

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.420
as we want with these things,
but the central idea here

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:03.250
is to provide our customers with
the online ordering facility

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:06.340
and the high quality
products, but also

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:10.390
surround them with these digital
services over the platform that

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:12.790
will help them with
their broader business.

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:16.480
And that's how we are planning
to differentiate ourselves

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.340
from those competitors
who are just selling

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:20.410
traditional products today.

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:23.620
With that, I'm going
to hand it off to Sam.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.570
SAM GANGA: Thanks, Serhat.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.190
I've heard this
story so many times.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:34.620
And it's just incredible that
here's a 100-year-old company--

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.370
well, it'll be 100
years next year, right?

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:37.120
SERHAT UNSAL: Yes.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.412
SAM GANGA: That's gone through
so many transformations.

00:16:39.412 --> 00:16:41.215
This is the fourth or
fifth transformation.

00:16:41.215 --> 00:16:43.090
If I remember, one of
the storylines that you

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.410
have that you talk about--

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.800
the first one
actually being when

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:49.780
we had the Great Depression.

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.370
The company reinvented
itself and is

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.230
going through so
many transformations.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:56.470
So this is a company
that had actually worked

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:59.890
through quite a bit
of change and is now

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:01.810
ready for the next frontier.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:04.780
So what I want to sort
of take you through is--

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.579
Serhat talked about
how entrenched

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:10.869
the entire value
proposition is with regards

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:12.950
to talking to the
customers, and frankly,

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.460
to the customers' customers,
which are consumers like you

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:18.588
and I, and really
building out, how

00:17:18.588 --> 00:17:19.880
do you actually relate to them?

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:21.047
How do you engage with them?

00:17:21.047 --> 00:17:22.500
How do you create
that stickiness?

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.270
But I want to unpack that
conceptual view, the value

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.050
proposition view and
the conceptual view,

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:31.570
which is the previous slide that
Serhat showed, and build on it,

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:33.910
and talk about what does
the logical model look

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:36.010
like when you unpack that?

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.260
And when you unpack that,
what does the physical model

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:40.810
look like, which is the
technical element needed

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:42.230
to make this a reality?

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.380
It's great to talk
about business agility,

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.900
but since it's so
entrenched in sort

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:49.510
of linking business and
technology together, linking

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:52.240
business, technology, and
digital capabilities together,

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:54.532
it's kind of incredible that
you had to bring all of us

00:17:54.532 --> 00:17:57.440
together to actually
deliver a value proposition.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.930
So I want to sort
of uncover that.

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:01.620
And when we get to the end of
the presentation, [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.570
will come up here
and do a quick demo

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:06.660
of where we see this going.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.680
But before I get into
it, I want to talk

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:11.260
about this journey a little
bit because the journey that we

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:14.595
started together about
eight, nine months ago

00:18:14.595 --> 00:18:16.720
didn't sort of happen with
the snap of the fingers.

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:18.012
There was a lot of interaction.

00:18:18.012 --> 00:18:22.150
There was a lot of working
with Serhat's Dawn leadership

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:25.355
team, the board, Bob's
teams, that Bob's obviously

00:18:25.355 --> 00:18:27.730
part of the leadership team,
but really getting everybody

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:29.620
to understand, what
is the value here?

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:31.030
Why should you make this pivot?

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:31.900
And why is it important?

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:33.650
It didn't happen with
the snap of fingers,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:36.093
as Serhat will
definitely tell you.

00:18:36.093 --> 00:18:37.510
So I want to sort
of walk through,

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:39.160
how do we actually go
through the process,

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:40.618
and what kind of
an approach did we

00:18:40.618 --> 00:18:42.820
use to help people
think through it?

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:44.500
Not going to spend
too much time on it,

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:48.820
but it is what we think of as
our transformation framework.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.540
This is business-led
digital transformation.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.960
I want to be very clear.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.922
And the journey
is what we really

00:18:55.922 --> 00:18:57.880
believe in, because this
is not going to happen

00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:59.075
in one year, two years.

00:18:59.075 --> 00:19:00.700
It actually takes a
little bit of time.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:03.670
And more importantly, it
needs to be sustainable.

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:06.200
You can't just expect that
this will happen in two years

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:06.670
and then you're done.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:07.670
It's not an initiative.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:10.070
It's a pivot for the whole
firm and the whole company.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:11.812
We call it the
connected enterprise.

00:19:11.812 --> 00:19:13.270
And really the
connected enterprise

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:14.687
has several key elements to it.

00:19:14.687 --> 00:19:16.270
And I'm going to go
through all of it.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.130
I'm just going to spend
about three or four

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:23.680
minutes going through the top
line capabilities, if you may.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:27.290
The first, Serhat spent a
lot of time talking about it,

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:32.190
which is the fact that the
customer agenda is actually

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.180
ready for disruption.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:35.370
Everything that we talk
about is customer-centric,

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:36.990
is outside in.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.910
The surveys that
Serhat talked about,

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:41.580
the interactions
with the customers,

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.730
in this case, the artisan
bakers, the Walmarts

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.620
of the world, and
their consumers

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.500
was very important to inform
what the strategy needs

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.090
to look like.

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:53.670
So once we had that
in place, really

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.040
started take a look at
what kind of experiences

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.040
do we need to create in
order to enable that today

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:00.220
and down the road?

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:03.180
So this notion of creating
experiences and customer

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:07.620
experiences that are going
to really effectively enable

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.320
the strategy that was created
was an extremely important part

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:10.560
of it.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.227
And, oh, by the way,
we were not looking

00:20:12.227 --> 00:20:15.090
at just products and
services that Dawn provided.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:17.510
We opened up the aperture
to say, what does

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.440
a customer really want from us?

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:23.520
And we created this
notion that we said

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:25.650
was more important
for you to understand

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.020
what experiences
the customer wants,

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.180
not what experiences
you could provide, still

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:31.800
an outside in perspective.

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:33.390
And then translating
that in terms

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.610
of organizational
capabilities to deliver

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.800
number one and number two.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:39.700
How do you deliver
profitable growth?

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.330
How do you deliver this from
an organizational capability?

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:43.500
Because nobody else
is going to do it.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:44.280
You have to do it.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:46.572
And you have to transform
your organization to do that.

00:20:46.572 --> 00:20:47.947
When you've looked
at all of this

00:20:47.947 --> 00:20:50.100
and you pivot into what
capabilities are necessary,

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.630
how do you break
down these silos

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.000
that we're normally
used to, whether it's

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:58.200
supply chain only this or
your customer service team

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:59.580
only does this?

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:02.260
How do you break down these
barriers between front, middle,

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:03.020
and back office?

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:05.370
And how do you
create an environment

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.070
that allows transformation
to happen more naturally?

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:11.980
That was really what we were
jointly working on together.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.443
And so there are eight
capabilities that we believe.

00:21:15.443 --> 00:21:16.860
And this is all
based on research,

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:20.680
by the way, that we've done
over the last three years,

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.200
not just from
talking to customers,

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:25.320
but working with external
parties who have actually

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.927
thought through what these
capabilities need to be.

00:21:27.927 --> 00:21:29.260
And they've done service for us.

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:30.100
We've done research.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:33.120
We really entrench that
research in terms of outcomes

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.602
and constantly refined it.

00:21:34.602 --> 00:21:36.810
I'm not going to go through
these eight capabilities,

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.150
just going to point out
a couple, three of them.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.250
The first one is
kind of obvious.

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:44.070
I think a lot of us are
here because we believe

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.070
that insight-driven
strategies and actions are

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:52.830
core to the way a business
would behave in a digital world.

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:55.260
What's interesting is that
one of the services KPMG

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:59.580
does on an annual basis
with CEOs across the globe,

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:01.560
we found that while
everybody sort of believes

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:05.190
that this is their
strategy, 78% of CEOs

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:08.460
actually don't use
data-driven insights

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:09.810
to inform their decisions.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.670
They still go with their gut.

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.200
I'm not suggesting
that gut is not

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.220
important for a
CEO in particular,

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:18.678
but this is something
that we are learning,

00:22:18.678 --> 00:22:20.220
and we're trying to
inform everybody.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.040
But this, we believe,
is core to one

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:25.170
of the capabilities required
for a transformation.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.170
Serhat is not one of
those CEOs, by the way.

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:28.908
[LAUGHING]

00:22:28.908 --> 00:22:30.450
But the second one,
of course, Serhat

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:32.617
talked about, which is the
innovation, and products,

00:22:32.617 --> 00:22:35.910
and services, not only
innovation of existing products

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:38.520
that you sell to your
customers, but also

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:42.180
digital twins that you
want to sell or enhance

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:43.710
your existing products with.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:45.540
Experience-centricity,
that's something

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:48.120
that is talked about a lot,
where your customers really

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.787
are the center of
your universe, and you

00:22:49.787 --> 00:22:52.702
want to deliver an experience,
not just a product for them.

00:22:52.702 --> 00:22:54.660
And this is not just for
the customer, but also

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.660
your employees so that
they're serving customers

00:22:56.660 --> 00:22:57.400
more effectively.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:22:59.980
And that's extremely
important as well.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.640
Seamless commerce and
seamless interactions, this

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:04.200
is about omnichannel
capabilities

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:05.898
so that if you
start a transaction,

00:23:05.898 --> 00:23:08.190
or if you start an inquiry,
or you start a conversation

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:10.560
on one channel, which
could be digital, that you

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:11.740
don't lose track of it.

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:14.240
You don't lose track of it when
somebody walks in the store,

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:18.150
or walks up to your sales
agent, or whoever it might be.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.510
The piece that is
a challenge as you

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:23.490
start to unfold all
these capabilities

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:26.155
is the response of
operations and supply chain.

00:23:26.155 --> 00:23:27.780
Supply chain is sort
of making a move--

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:29.988
and we believe this is an
important move-- from being

00:23:29.988 --> 00:23:33.690
make-centric and cost-centric
to be customer-centric

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.360
and commercially oriented,
sort of moving upstream

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:38.730
so that you're not just
thinking of yourself as, I got

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.563
to deliver the best
product, create the best

00:23:40.563 --> 00:23:42.130
product at the cheapest price.

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:44.160
And so we can go on
and on through this.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.770
Suffice it to say that
we believe, obviously,

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:50.730
the alignment of people, talent,
whether it's growing talent

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:52.800
internally, changing
the way people think,

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:55.270
or externally hiring,
talent is very important.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.870
And finally, a digitally-enabled
architecture, which we

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:00.100
will talk about in a
little bit more detail.

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:02.840
So I'm not going to
delve into details there.

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.590
Serhat already talked
about the importance

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:06.240
of the partner
ecosystem and the fact

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:08.550
that you want to
welcome partners.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:12.150
And what's very refreshing
about this is none of this

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:14.505
is sort of behind
some firewall setting.

00:24:14.505 --> 00:24:16.755
And Serhat and Bob go out
and talk to their customers,

00:24:16.755 --> 00:24:17.713
talk to their partners.

00:24:17.713 --> 00:24:19.600
They're very open about
what they want to do,

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.960
which is sort of what
Serhat calls the open source

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:23.345
world of businesses.

00:24:23.345 --> 00:24:24.720
You want to sort
of share as much

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:26.428
as you can so that
you're inviting people

00:24:26.428 --> 00:24:27.540
into your ecosystem.

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.340
And you're creating
possibilities

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:30.650
that you never knew possible.

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:33.150
And so that's something that
we want, creating opportunities

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:35.010
that you never knew possible.

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:36.500
So one of the
things I'll end with

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.860
is that as we started to
unpack this more and more,

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:42.860
we found that as
we talked about it

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:46.460
and began to monitor companies
that were using this,

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:49.520
companies that use this kind
of a framework, not exactly

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:52.370
the framework, but really
invested in those capabilities

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:55.130
were twice as likely to
succeed in their transformation

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:56.490
efforts.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:59.330
And this is something that,
whether you believe in exactly

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:02.195
all those capabilities or you
want to modify some of this,

00:25:02.195 --> 00:25:03.890
this is directionally
where we believe

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:07.010
companies will need to go in
order to survive and thrive

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:08.330
in the digital age.

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:10.160
And this is where,
when Serhat opened,

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.680
we really are trying to link
those two together and saying,

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:15.230
there is no business strategy
and digital strategy.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:16.910
It's one and the same thing.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:19.010
And you have to understand
how they work together

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:21.110
and how you would
sustain them together.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:24.680
So with that said, and as a
backdrop for how we actually

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:26.540
went through this
journey, I want

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:29.270
to sort of transition a little
bit into where we left off

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:31.880
with our story and
Serhat talking about,

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:34.790
what does Dawn look like
as a platform company?

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:38.510
Conceptually, these are the
products, services, areas,

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:41.530
categories that we
believe is important,

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:45.230
that Dawn believes is very
important for futureproofing

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:46.430
the business.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:48.950
At the heart of this, of
course, is all the data

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.820
that you're going to collect.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.520
This is a fairly
seasonal business.

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:56.015
Bakers have to react.

00:25:56.015 --> 00:25:58.390
There's a lot of bakery business
all throughout the year,

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:00.810
but when they get into
Thanksgiving or now coming up

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:03.032
with Easter,
there's a big spike.

00:26:03.032 --> 00:26:04.740
When are they actually
looking for labor?

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.210
What kind of labor
are they looking for?

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:07.793
How often do they
look for that labor?

00:26:07.793 --> 00:26:09.765
What can they pay for it?

00:26:09.765 --> 00:26:11.170
You are collecting that data.

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.890
Even if you anonymize it,
it's very powerful data.

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:17.670
And so each one of these bubbles
or clouds that you look at

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.810
creates and puts
out so much data

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:22.920
that we believe that
is actually how we're

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:27.000
going to keep this a
sustainable, competitive edge

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.830
for Dawn and for their bakers.

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:31.645
So let's sort of unpack and
go from a conceptual view

00:26:31.645 --> 00:26:33.270
into a more of a
logical view, and then

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:35.190
into a technology view.

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.260
So if you look at this
a little differently,

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:40.050
in order for us to be able
to create what we think

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:41.970
of as a plug and
play architecture,

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.980
there are two
fundamental elements

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:45.900
that are necessary,
even before you get

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.420
down to collecting the data.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:51.660
To be able to create these
business services that Serhat

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:55.200
talked about, whether it's
connecting up with Indeed,

00:26:55.200 --> 00:27:00.305
or a gig economy provider of
resources, or whoever you have,

00:27:00.305 --> 00:27:01.680
there's two things
that we wanted

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:03.190
to sort of ensure that we do.

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:05.850
The first one was
working together.

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.070
The Dawn team said,
it needs to be

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.785
a microservices-based
architecture.

00:27:09.785 --> 00:27:11.160
Whether you're
building your own,

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.080
you're plugging something
in, you essentially

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.330
need the ability to
plug into a chassis.

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.190
And that microservices
architecture

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:19.540
has to enable that.

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:23.850
The second piece is that using
APIs that are readily available

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:27.390
is an excellent way to
accelerate, and frankly,

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:30.420
not only accelerate but also
run test and learn cycles.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:33.120
And we're doing some of that and
exploring that as we go along.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.980
When we go into the details,
we'll talk about some of that.

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:38.795
But all of this, whatever
the areas are that Bob

00:27:38.795 --> 00:27:39.670
talked about in the--

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:41.837
I'm sorry, Serhat talked
about in the previous slide

00:27:41.837 --> 00:27:45.060
with operations and marketing
technology knowledge,

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:48.030
et cetera, what we
believe is that you

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:52.470
would need these to plug in to a
technology backbone, what we're

00:27:52.470 --> 00:27:55.740
calling the core business
platform for Dawn, Dawn Core

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.580
Business Platform.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:59.650
So what does that look like?

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:03.910
Today, that is built on
a Google GCP backbone.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:05.590
Think of that as the chassis.

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:09.120
The chassis is where everything
is going to plug into.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.800
And the chassis obviously
has core capabilities, which

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:13.310
we'll talk about in a second.

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:14.888
But it also has
business solutions.

00:28:14.888 --> 00:28:16.930
We don't think of YouTube
as a business solution.

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:21.880
We just think of it as a cool
place to watch videos roughly.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:23.650
But Dawn has already
started the process

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:25.390
of creating a Dawn channel.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:27.370
Somebody knows,
what does it look

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:32.620
like when you're creating a
very specific product or a end

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:34.240
product for your customer?

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.750
And what do you need in order
to be able to create that?

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:37.900
Because this is an art for them.

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:38.817
This is their passion.

00:28:38.817 --> 00:28:42.280
They want to learn about
new techniques for frosting,

00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:44.460
or to create a new
kind of a cake,

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:47.650
or what's coming up in the
food services industry that

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:49.310
is relevant to them.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.220
So when we look at
this, we are actually

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:54.130
looking at plugging in things
that are fairly native to what

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:55.300
Google already does.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:57.383
And we're calling those
Google Business Solutions.

00:28:57.383 --> 00:28:59.925
It's still part of Google Cloud,
but it's a business solution

00:28:59.925 --> 00:29:00.700
that is provided.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:01.720
It could be AdWords.

00:29:01.720 --> 00:29:03.680
It could be Express
Shopping List.

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:08.373
It could be Recommendations
AI that we're going to use.

00:29:08.373 --> 00:29:09.790
But there are
things that you have

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:11.890
to always create by yourself.

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:14.020
One of them is the
commerce engine.

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:17.740
We do believe that in order to
achieve the goals that Serhat

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:20.770
talked about, you are going to
need to transact fundamentally.

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:24.130
Well, that's not native in the
Google Cloud, yet at least.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:25.930
But we will be using
a commerce engine

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:27.852
that we will plug and
play from elsewhere.

00:29:27.852 --> 00:29:30.310
We will be creating our own
customer service transformation

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:31.450
interaction models.

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:33.670
We will be creating things
that are specific to what

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:36.550
Dawn needs, curated by
Dawn, created by Dawn,

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:39.670
but they will all ultimately
plug into the same chassis.

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:43.030
And we believe that's
extremely important for us.

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:45.740
When you sort of unpack
this a little bit more,

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:49.420
what we're seeing is
that we're going to use--

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:53.055
I mean, this is not a technical
deep dive presentation,

00:29:53.055 --> 00:29:55.180
but I'm going to do the
best I can to sort of cover

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:56.497
the top elements.

00:29:56.497 --> 00:29:58.330
You are going to have
a serverless computing

00:29:58.330 --> 00:29:58.872
architecture.

00:29:58.872 --> 00:30:00.520
You are going to have
an elastic cloud.

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:03.850
You are going to have all
those things that are normal,

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:06.677
expected, container engines,
whatever you want to call it.

00:30:06.677 --> 00:30:08.010
You are going to do all of that.

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:09.900
And that's sort of
the top line item.

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:11.150
You are going to use BigQuery.

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:12.740
You're going to do all of that.

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:14.860
But there are elements
that you need in order

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:17.110
to deliver this business
agility, which will only

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:19.930
come from really thinking
about your ability

00:30:19.930 --> 00:30:24.550
to not only subscribe to
APIs, but also publish APIs.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:26.555
So as much as you're
subscribing to an API--

00:30:26.555 --> 00:30:28.180
and we'll talk about
that in a second--

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:31.150
we're also going to publish APIs
that other people might use.

00:30:31.150 --> 00:30:34.065
Maybe it's the organizer
of this event that needs--

00:30:34.065 --> 00:30:36.190
I don't know how many donuts
we had in the morning.

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:37.660
Maybe we didn't have any donuts.

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:40.450
But maybe they need
2,000 donuts a day.

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:42.400
They could subscribe
to this and find a way

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:45.610
to engage with artisanal
bakers to deliver those 2,000

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:48.040
donuts a day in the morning.

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.280
So when we look at the
enabling technologies

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:54.820
and what it takes to be
able to really unpack this,

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:56.720
there are two elements to it.

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:01.810
The first is the
ability to sort of have

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:03.838
a conversational interface.

00:31:03.838 --> 00:31:05.630
And that's what we're
using Dialogflow for.

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:08.620
I'm going to literally just not
talk about the top row there

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.900
because I think we already
have enough sessions that

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:12.070
cover those.

00:31:12.070 --> 00:31:14.510
We're going to talk about the
business elements of this.

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:16.030
The first one is Dialogflow.

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.780
And we'll talk a little
bit more about it,

00:31:17.780 --> 00:31:20.080
but Dialogflow is essentially
the interaction engine

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:22.700
for conversational commerce,
extremely powerful.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:26.260
If you don't know about it, I
would recommend you learn more

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:29.530
about it because it really
brings across the notion

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:34.280
that whether you're interacting
with a customer using Google

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:36.280
Home, or whether you're
using your mobile phone,

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:38.405
or whether you're using
some other element that you

00:31:38.405 --> 00:31:41.600
don't know about today, it
will be extremely powerful.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.660
The second that we
found very valuable

00:31:43.660 --> 00:31:46.060
is the ability to
store session data.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:52.090
Because your performance
of your conversation

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.550
is important, and
the speech to text,

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:57.438
the text to speech
elements are important.

00:31:57.438 --> 00:31:59.230
You will need to have
a caching capability.

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:01.390
And we're using
Firebase for that.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:03.520
Cloud communications,
we use Twilio

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:07.030
to be able to start using
the ability to alert people

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:09.178
quickly, get them informed.

00:32:09.178 --> 00:32:10.720
And if you don't
know Twilio, I would

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.620
recommend you get to know them.

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:13.870
Now you're going to get into--

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:15.790
I'm going to talk about
the commerce engine

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:17.710
next, which is commercetools.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:23.590
It's a ground up microservices
architecture-based commerce

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:25.000
engine.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:26.690
We found it to be
extremely powerful,

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:29.050
easy to plug and play,
easy to get off the ground,

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:32.620
and really curate your overall
interactive experience.

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:36.510
And commercetools
is, we believe,

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:38.020
one of the vendors
out there that's

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:39.520
doing a great amount
of work around,

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.050
how do you actually
plug into more

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:46.240
of a cloud-based, native
microservices architecture?

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:48.750
Now this interesting part
that I left out in the middle

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:50.610
is actually one of
the elements where

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:52.080
we believe we're
going to generate

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.220
a lot of business interest.

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:56.160
This is what we think
of as one aspect

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:58.100
of our fulfillment engine.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.180
Now one of the things
that came out--

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:02.235
and we're obviously
learning from the market,

00:33:02.235 --> 00:33:04.500
and is a huge test and learn
cycle that works pretty

00:33:04.500 --> 00:33:05.340
quickly--

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:10.890
is that last mile delivery
for fulfillment is important.

00:33:10.890 --> 00:33:12.690
The example that
you used, Serhat, I

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:16.270
got to deliver 500 cupcakes.

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:19.290
Now I need the
ingredients tomorrow.

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:21.480
How are you going
to get it to me?

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:23.970
You can either use
Dawn's logistics network

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.200
to be able to deliver
it, which is not

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:29.880
meant for last mile delivery.

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:31.830
Or you can engage by
plugging and playing

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:35.760
Postmates or somebody
else to be able to do it.

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:37.680
And what we're
saying is eventually

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:39.805
we expect that
these partnerships,

00:33:39.805 --> 00:33:41.430
whether it's a Postmate
or whether it's

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:44.040
somebody else,
Curbside, what have you,

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:45.900
you're going to
start using these

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:49.410
to be able to actually deliver
products in the last mile.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.600
And we're going to see that
when we get to the demo.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:53.040
We've actually plugged that in.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:56.140
What we also found is that
it's not a one size fits all.

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:57.870
You could use
Postmates in New York.

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:59.670
You could use Curbside in Miami.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:02.230
You could use your own
network in Chicago.

00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:03.960
You can order whatever you want.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:05.910
Ultimately, you have
the ability to plug

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:09.150
in business APIs,
business services,

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:11.310
and rapidly get
it off the ground.

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:13.260
In terms of, of
course, when you're

00:34:13.260 --> 00:34:14.760
looking at these
business APIs, it's

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:18.500
complicated because there are
a lot of operational factors.

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:20.969
What used to be the longest
pole in the tent, which

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:24.378
is connect up the whole
infrastructure from Postmates,

00:34:24.378 --> 00:34:25.920
connect up this,
make sure it works--

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:27.699
we got it done in a week.

00:34:27.699 --> 00:34:31.270
At least the first shot at
it, we got it done in a week.

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:33.460
And that was actually a
big revelation for us,

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:35.760
because it is about
the speed of business.

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:38.125
This is about the clock
speed of business.

00:34:38.125 --> 00:34:39.750
And if technology
doesn't support that,

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:40.750
we've got a big problem.

00:34:40.750 --> 00:34:42.458
And this is really
what it's meant to do,

00:34:42.458 --> 00:34:44.590
is to accelerate that process.

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:47.520
For those of you sort of
interested in the architecture

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:48.960
diagram, we didn't disappoint.

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:52.093
So we do have one of the more
complex ones that I've seen.

00:34:52.093 --> 00:34:53.760
It's actually a very
simple one compared

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:56.230
to where we are going to be.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:34:58.990
But this is setting up
the demo a little bit,

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.110
just to give you a flavor for--

00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:03.180
I'm not going to steal
[INAUDIBLE] thunder here,

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:06.210
but really what I want
to communicate here

00:35:06.210 --> 00:35:11.100
is the fact that we have
separated out the modalities

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:15.150
of interaction from the APIs.

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:19.980
The APIs is actually where, in
some sense, the magic happens.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:21.917
And you can look at
it as, those of you

00:35:21.917 --> 00:35:24.000
who are technologists, and
say plugging in the API

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:26.420
is extremely important,
the resiliency of the API,

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:29.890
the performance of
the service itself,

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:31.680
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:34.225
But we're seeing that
a lot of the magic

00:35:34.225 --> 00:35:35.850
is beginning to happen
and will happen,

00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:38.550
continue to happen on the front
end because of the devices

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:41.603
that are proliferating,
businesses.

00:35:41.603 --> 00:35:43.020
We foresee a
situation where there

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:46.148
could be a Google device sitting
at every artisanal baker.

00:35:46.148 --> 00:35:47.940
And they are going to
actually use a device

00:35:47.940 --> 00:35:51.150
to interact with Dawn,
not sort of get on a phone

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:52.655
or do something else.

00:35:52.655 --> 00:35:54.030
We believe that
that is extremely

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:57.930
important for the future of this
platform that is being created.

00:35:57.930 --> 00:35:59.388
And [INAUDIBLE]
will go through why

00:35:59.388 --> 00:36:01.805
we believe that's the case
from an experience perspective,

00:36:01.805 --> 00:36:03.870
speaking about the
connected enterprise story

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:06.610
and how important
experiences are part of it.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:10.030
For the purposes
of our conversation

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:12.910
today, one of the things
I want to point out

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:14.745
is the entire API gateway--

00:36:14.745 --> 00:36:16.120
I shouldn't say
the entire piece.

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:16.930
Let me clarify.

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:20.030
The API gateway is
based on Apigee.

00:36:20.030 --> 00:36:22.470
Now not every API is
available through Apigee,

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:26.610
so sometimes you have to go
directly to the provider,

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:29.540
to the service provider and get
the API, and connect to them.

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:31.157
Commercetools is
an example of that.

00:36:31.157 --> 00:36:33.240
It's not available-- at
least when we shut it off,

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.250
it wasn't available
as a part of Apigee.

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:36.603
But Postmates is.

00:36:36.603 --> 00:36:38.520
And we accelerated our
process because we used

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.590
Apigee to connect the back end.

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:46.380
So obviously, this
is a drill down

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:48.160
off the actual
architecture that we have.

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:49.577
There's a bigger
architecture that

00:36:49.577 --> 00:36:52.500
brings in a lot of the machine
learning capabilities, the data

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:55.140
capabilities that are extremely
important for the growth

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:56.100
of this.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:58.350
So with that, I'm going to
hand it over to [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:00.130
to actually go through the demo.

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:05.798
And then we'll have about seven
